\description{
Makes a plot that shows the raw data points, a violin (the distribution), the
mean, and error bars for the mean, for the conditions of an experiment. By
default it draws all of those things, but you can turn on and off each element
(\code{dots}, \code{violin}, \code{bars}, \code{line}, \code{error_bars}) and change the layout.
Plots are saved as a pdf in \code{outp}, and returned as a ggplot that you can
customise further. \code{mypirate} is an older name for the same function.
}
\details{
\strong{Specifying the conditions}
\itemize{
\item \code{x_condition} is the column shown along the x axis, \code{colour_condition} splits
by colour, and \code{facet_condition} makes a separate panel for each level.
\code{plot_condition} is a shortcut where you give a vector of up to three
names, in the order colour, x, facet.
\item If you give only \code{colour_condition} it is also used for the x axis.
\item \code{dv} is the column with the data to plot. If you give several columns, they
are stacked into a column called \code{variable}, which you can use as a
condition.
\item Data should be in long format, with a row for each observation (for example
each trial), and a column identifying participants (\code{pid}).
Set \code{pid_average = TRUE} to average each participant's data within
conditions before plotting.
\item If the data has a column called \code{use}, only rows where \code{use} is 1 are
plotted, unless you set \code{useall = TRUE}.
}

\strong{Normalising}: \code{norm_by = "dv"} converts values to z scores for each of the
dvs, so that you can compare them on one axis. \code{norm_by = "pid"} does that for
each participant. Use \code{norm_scale = "iq"} for a scale with mean 100 and
standard deviation 15 instead.

\strong{Saving}: the file name is made from the title, the dv and the names of the
conditions. Set \code{outp = NULL} to avoid saving a file.

\strong{Old arguments} \code{cond}, \code{cond2}, \code{facetby}, \code{norm}, \code{norm_iq}, \code{norm_pid} are
kept so that old code still works. Use \code{x_condition}, \code{colour_condition},
\code{facet_condition}, \code{norm_by} and \code{norm_scale} in new code.
}
